Derive storage from a charge voltage relation (@physics-capacitive-work: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "State-the-physical-assumptions-behind-a-lumped-circuit.lingua#r_001A7088BC509FE999669C908D") { r_0044E82DC1FD162C1B4C9F03EA
An ideal linear capacitor has a signed plate charge Q and voltage difference v related by Q=Cv, with C>0 fixed by the declared geometry and material state. The other plate has −Q in this two-terminal model. C has units coulombs per volt, or farads. It is a response coefficient within a domain, not the maximum charge, maximum voltage, or a certification of a real device. Zero Q and v leave the same C defined by the relation; division of an observed zero by zero would not measure it.

With current entering the chosen positive-voltage terminal, I=Q′=Cv′ if C is constant. From p=vI, the change in stored energy is dU=v dQ=(Q/C)dQ. Integrating from the uncharged state gives U=Q²/(2C)=Cv²/2≥0. This remains positive for either charge sign. It is the reversible electrical storage of the specified ideal element, not necessarily all the work supplied by a source during a dissipative charging process. It also does not automatically include chemical storage, mechanical work or dielectric loss.

The constant-C assumption matters. If geometry changes, differentiating Q=Cv gives I=Cv′+vC′, and the electric storage derivative need not equal port power because mechanical work can cross the system boundary. If a nonlinear reversible relation v(Q) is specified, the appropriate storage difference is ∫v(Q)dQ; replacing it by Qv/2 requires a linear relation through the origin. Hysteresis or leakage needs further state and dissipation information. Do not generalize the quadratic formula by its appearance alone.

Parallel ideal capacitors with the same terminal voltage have Q_total=(C₁+C₂)v and summed energy (C₁+C₂)v²/2. An initially neutral floating internal node between two series capacitors imposes equal signed transferred charge magnitudes, so v=Q/C₁+Q/C₂ and C_series=C₁C₂/(C₁+C₂). If that internal node has a nonzero initial net charge, its constraint must be retained and the simple zero-offset reduction is insufficient. These reductions follow from specific connections and charge accounts rather than from the shapes of symbols.

For two separately charged ideal capacitors, adding their stored energies is valid in the declared independent-element approximation. Stray mutual capacitance and the surrounding field can require a larger relation between conductor charges and potentials. The familiar parallel-plate estimate C=ε₀A/d further assumes a suitable vacuum geometry with negligible edge effects; deriving that field relation belongs to the fuller Gauss-law formation. The work derivation above needs only the stipulated linear charge–voltage relation and does not depend on an unproved general field-energy density formula.

**Work and reading:** derive the quadratic storage using signed Q, then check Q=−Cv₀. Compare a 2 μF capacitor at 5 V (25 μJ) with a 3 μF capacitor at 0 V. Determine each charge and energy before any connection is made. Show why neutral total charge does not imply zero energy. Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) 8.1 and 8.3 progressively. Qualify the opening phrase about a largest or maximum charge per volt: the common C is the linear response coefficient, while device ratings require separate evidence. Use the work integral to establish the formula before studying the broader field-energy claims and source exercises.

